In the <s>Itihāsas</s> and <s>Purāṇas</s> as well, in the contexts dealing with creation, sustenance, and dissolution, this alone is understood to be the Supreme Reality. As in the <s>Mahābhārata</s>, when asked:
'From what was all this created — this world of moving and non-moving things? And into what does it enter at dissolution? Tell us, O Grandfather' —
he declares:
'<s>Nārāyaṇa</s>, the cosmic form, the infinite Self, the eternal one' —
and further:
'The sages, the ancestors, the gods, the great elements, the constituents, the moving and non-moving — this entire world is born from <s>Nārāyaṇa</s>.'
And the <s>Vaiṣṇava Purāṇa</s> (Viṣṇu Purāṇa), unanimously accepted by all the authoritative learned ones from the east, north, south, and west as being sufficient for establishing all <s>dharma</s> and all reality — from this it is understood that <s>Brahman</s> is the cause of the world's origin and the rest, as stated: 'From whom is the origin and the rest of this [universe].' It is universally agreed that this [Purāṇa], beginning with the question 'What is the cause of its origin and the rest?' and answering 'It arose from <s>Viṣṇu</s>,' is exclusively devoted to establishing the specific nature of <s>Brahman</s>. As stated therein:
'<s>Prakṛti</s>, which is known as having both manifest and unmanifest forms, and <s>Puruṣa</s> too — both of these dissolve into the Supreme Self (<s>Paramātman</s>);
The Supreme Self, the support of all, the Supreme Lord, is called by the name <s>Viṣṇu</s> in the <s>Vedas</s> and <s>Vedāntas</s>.'
The meaning is: in all the <s>Vedas</s> and <s>Vedāntas</s>, by all words denoting the Supreme Cause, it is He alone who is celebrated. Just as the <s>Nārāyaṇānuvāka</s> is devoted in all the <s>śrutis</s> exclusively to establishing the specific nature of the Supreme <s>Brahman</s>, so too is this <s>Vaiṣṇava Purāṇa</s> —
'I wish to hear from you, O knower of <s>Dharma</s>, how the world came into being and how it will be hereafter, O greatly blessed one;
Of what the world consists in <s>Brahman</s>, and this moving and non-moving [world] — how it was dissolved, where, and where it will [again] dissolve' —
thus beginning with 'What is the Supreme <s>Brahman</s>?' it proceeds:
'The world arose from <s>Viṣṇu</s> and is established in Him; He is the agent of its sustenance and dissolution, and He is the world itself;
The highest of the high, the Supreme, the Supreme Self, self-established, free from designation by form, color, and the like;
Free from decay, destruction, transformation, growth, and birth — He can be described only as "He exists" (<s>sat asti</s>);
Since He dwells everywhere and everything dwells in Him — therefore He is called <s>Vāsudeva</s> by the wise;
That <s>Brahman</s> is supreme, eternal, unborn, imperishable, immutable, always of one nature, and pure because of the absence of anything to be rejected;
That very [<s>Brahman</s>] is all this, possessing both manifest and unmanifest forms, and abides also in the form of <s>Puruṣa</s> and in the form of Time;
He has transcended the material nature of all beings, the modifications, the qualities and other defects, O sage; He has transcended all coverings; the Self of all — by Him is pervaded all that is within the worlds;
He whose nature consists of all auspicious qualities, who brings forth the classes of beings by a fraction of His own power, who assumes a desired exalted body by His own will, who has accomplished the welfare of the entire world;
A mass of splendor, strength, sovereignty, supreme knowledge, heroism, power, and other qualities — the highest of the high, in whom no afflictions and the like exist, the Lord of higher and lower;
He is the Lord, of both individual and collective form, both unmanifest and manifest in nature; the Lord of all, the all-seeing, the all-knowing, possessing all powers, called the Supreme Lord;
That by which is known what is free from defect, pure, supreme, stainless, of one form — or is seen or is attained — that is knowledge; anything other than that is declared to be ignorance.'
Thus [the Viṣṇu Purāṇa] is devoted exclusively to determining the specific nature of the Supreme <s>Brahman</s>.

If [it is asked]: how is it known that the concealment of the [self's] false form is the <s>karma</s> of the field-knower (<s>kṣetrajña</s>)? — From the statement:
'The third power is accepted, called <s>avidyā</s>, also known as <s>karma</s>;
That field-knower power (<s>kṣetrajñaśakti</s>), O king, enveloped and all-pervading,
obtains all the continuous afflictions of <s>saṃsāra</s>;
and because it is concealed by that [<s>avidyā</s>]...'
and so forth.

Thus it has been established that <s>Nārāyaṇa</s>, who is the Supreme <s>Brahman</s>, knowable through the <s>Veda</s> with its subsidiary sciences supplemented by the <s>Itihāsas</s>, <s>Purāṇas</s>, and <s>Dharmaśāstras</s>, is the antithesis of everything to be rejected; utterly distinct from all other entities; whose sole nature is unlimited knowledge and bliss; the treasure-house of innumerable, innate, unsurpassable auspicious qualities; whose sentient and non-sentient entities — differing in nature, existence, and activity — all conform to His will; whose infinite great sovereign glory is of immeasurable nature and essence; and who has the infinite phenomenal world of diverse sentient and non-sentient beings as the instrument of His play (<s>līlā</s>). [The texts:] 'All this indeed is <s>Brahman</s>' — 'All this has That as its self' — 'You are That, <s>Śvetaketu</s>' —
'Some call Him <s>Agni</s>, others <s>Marut</s>, others <s>Prajāpati</s>;
some <s>Indra</s>, others <s>Prāṇa</s>, still others the eternal <s>Brahman</s>' —
'The luminaries and all that is bright in the world, the three worlds, the world-protectors, the triple <s>Veda</s>,
the three sacred fires and the five oblations — all the gods are but the son of <s>Devakī</s>' —
'You are the sacrifice, You are the <s>vaṣaṭkāra</s>, You are the <s>Oṃkāra</s>, O scorcher of foes;
You are the abode of the seasons, the first of the <s>Vasus</s>, You are <s>Prajāpati</s>' —
'The entire world is Your body; the stable earth is Your steadfastness;
Fire is Your anger, <s>Soma</s> is Your grace, O bearer of the <s>Śrīvatsa</s> mark' —
'The luminaries are <s>Viṣṇu</s>, the worlds are <s>Viṣṇu</s>, the forests are <s>Viṣṇu</s>, the mountains and directions too;
the rivers and oceans — He alone is everything, that which exists and that which does not, O best of the twice-born' —
In all such usages of grammatical co-reference (<s>sāmānādhikaraṇya</s>), it has been stated that by all words, through having everything as His body, <s>Brahman</s> alone is denoted in every way. The Supreme <s>Brahman</s>, of true resolve (<s>satyasaṃkalpa</s>), resolving 'Let Me become manifold,' Himself differentiates the subtle elemental matter in its accumulated collective form, along with the multitude of experiencers, which have dissolved into Himself; from that subtle elemental matter He creates the gross elements; into them He enters as the Self of the class of experiencers; by those gross elements presided over by sentient souls and mutually combined, He fashions the entire world; and He Himself also enters into everything as the Self of all, being established as the Supreme Self (<s>Paramātman</s>), having everything as His body, abiding in manifold ways. That which is this subtle elemental matter is what is denoted by the word <s>Prakṛti</s>. The multitude of experiencers alone is what is called by the word <s>Puruṣa</s>. And those two — <s>Prakṛti</s> and <s>Puruṣa</s> — being the body of the Supreme Self, are modes (<s>prakāra</s>) of the Supreme Self. Having those as His modes, it is the Supreme Self alone who is denoted by the words <s>Prakṛti</s> and <s>Puruṣa</s>. 'He desired: "Let Me become many, let Me be born"' — 'Having created that, He Himself entered into it' — 'Having entered into it, He became both the defined and the undefined, the founded and the unfounded, the conscious and the unconscious, the true and the untrue — He became the true' — all that was stated earlier is made clear by this very <s>śruti</s>.
